= BITwiki organization =

BITwiki is organized so knowledge can be authored coherently, decomposed where reuse is useful, stored semantically, computed deterministically, traversed through explicit relationships, entered through multiple interfaces, and audited through sources/provenance/revision history.

== The architecture in one view ==

<pre>
                         KNOWLEDGE OBJECT
                               │
       ┌───────────────────────┼────────────────────────┐
       │                       │                        │
    IDENTITY                CONTENT                 EPISTEMICS
       │                       │                        │
 Entity type               Book Matter              Evidence
 Domain(s)                 Overview                 Sources
 Relationships             Theory / model           Provenance
 Scope                     Mechanism                Standing
                            Methods                  Uncertainty
                            Applications             Review
                            References               Lifecycle
       │                       │                        │
       └───────────────────────┼────────────────────────┘
                               │
                      STRUCTURED WIKI STATE
                     SMW graph · Cargo records
                               │
                    SCRIBUNTO / LUA COMPILER
       normalize · type-check · bounded derive · project · diagnose
                               │
                         NAVIGATION / VIEWS
       Portal · Category · Outline · List · Index · Query · Graph
                               │
                       INTERACTION SURFACES
                 MediaWiki UI · BIThub / Discourse
                               │
                         READER DEPTH
                  Simple · Core · Technical · Frontier
</pre>

No branch in this diagram is the whole system. See [[BITwiki:Programmable knowledge substrate]] and [[BITwiki:Lua architecture]].

== MediaWiki primitives ==

=== Page ===
A durable knowledge object or project/documentation unit.

=== Namespace ===
A broad system/page-class boundary with MediaWiki behavior. Namespace is not subject taxonomy.

=== Category ===
A human-readable browse/index surface. Category hierarchy is a navigation graph, not the ontology.

=== Template ===
Reusable/transcluded declarative structure, interface, semantic-emission, or stable invocation pattern. Non-trivial reusable procedural logic may delegate to Lua.

=== Property ===
Semantic MediaWiki attribute or relationship with explicitly documented meaning. Property assertions are durable graph state; a computed display is not automatically another assertion.

=== Concept ===
A stored semantic query/computed collection when that capability provides value. It is not synonymous with V2 entity type <code>Concept</code>.

=== Module ===
Scribunto/Lua reusable executable logic. In V2, <code>Module:BITwiki/*</code> is the deterministic compiler/type-system/bounded-inference/projection layer over authored and structured wiki state.

=== Cargo table ===
Repeated structured operational state when a table-shaped lifecycle is clearer than page-level semantic identity. Cargo does not replace SMW as the canonical graph.

=== Redirect ===
Title continuity and migration mechanism. Redirects should represent deliberate equivalence/continuity decisions, not hide unresolved source differences.

=== Revision / history / diff ===
Native provenance and correction trail. Do not recreate this data in semantic fields without a concrete reason.

== Execution and interface layers ==

<pre>
wikitext / Templates + SMW + Cargo
              ↓
      MediaWiki + Scribunto/Lua
              ↓
normalize → type-check → resolve → bounded derive → project → diagnose
              ↓
MediaWiki views / graph / diagnostics / machine-facing models
              ↕
authenticated API / plugin / service / agent boundary
              ↕
BIThub / Discourse
</pre>

BITwiki/MediaWiki remains the canonical durable knowledge plane. BIThub/Discourse is the conversation, coordination, active-inference and workflow plane. BITCORE/agent systems may perform external research, synthesis and orchestration, but they do not silently acquire authority over canonical wiki state.

Scribunto is not the cross-service transport. Credentials, networking, queues, retries and background execution belong to explicit integration infrastructure.

== Independent dimensions ==

<pre>
namespace
≠ entity type
≠ domain
≠ Book Matter
≠ epistemic standing
≠ lifecycle
≠ relationship
≠ navigation view
≠ reader depth
≠ interface
≠ compiler-derived presentation
</pre>

This separation is the central V2 organizational correction.

== Ontology ==

[[BITwiki:Ontology]] defines what kinds of objects and relations the knowledge graph represents.

[[BITwiki:Entity types]] contains the current evolving base vocabulary. Types are stable recognition patterns, not rigid bins.

[[BITwiki:Relationships]] defines semantic edges conservatively. Specific relation semantics should not be inferred from proximity, portal ancestry, category membership, or interface co-occurrence.

[[BITwiki:Semantic properties]] documents what V2 actually implements in the Property namespace.

Compiler-facing controlled vocabulary and deterministic relationship-display metadata are repository-authoritative in <code>bitwiki-runtime-schema.json</code> and projected to Scribunto through <code>Module:BITwiki/Data/Schema</code>. That runtime schema is not the whole ontology and does not replace SMW Property contracts.

== Domains ==

Domain answers: '''what field/body of knowledge does this belong to?'''

Current controlled Domains:

* Systems science
* Science
* Biology
* Computer science
* Mathematics
* Philosophy
* Technology
* Electronics
* Energy
* Engineering
* Chemistry
* Physics
* Medicine

These thirteen working Domains are not a claim that all knowledge fits into thirteen exclusive branches. A page may span Domains. Expansion should come from recurring corpus/coverage need; see [[BITwiki:Requested knowledge]] and [[BITwiki:Navigation and coverage research]].

== Book Matter ==

[[BITwiki:Book Matter]] is the reusable anatomy of knowledge. A coherent parent page may expose meaningful portions such as Overview/Definition, Concepts/Frameworks, Mechanism/Methods, Evidence, Applications/Case studies, Debate/controversy, Timeline, References/bibliography, or specialized maps.

Split content only when the part has independent reuse/retrieval/provenance value.

'''Modularity does not mean fragmentation.'''

== Epistemics ==

[[BITwiki:Epistemics]] separates epistemic standing, evidence, source type/evaluation, provenance, confidence rationale, uncertainty, review/verification, lifecycle, and perspective. A single status field or numeric score must not stand in for the full question of how something is known.

Lua may validate or diagnose explicit epistemic structure but must not manufacture epistemic standing from page shape, source prestige, or interface context.

== Navigation ==

Navigation is a plural set of views over the knowledge substrate.

=== Domain portals ===
Broad reader-facing entry points tied to accepted Domains, with semantic queries plus curated orientation.

=== Focused / recursive portals ===
Focused portals represent coherent routes into the corpus without automatically becoming Domains.

Examples:

* [[Portal:Bioinformatics]] — navigational parents Biology + Computer science
* [[Portal:Python]] — navigational parent Computer science

Older slash paths may redirect for continuity, but portal filesystem nesting is not ontology inheritance.

=== Categories ===
Browse/index groupings. Subcategories may have multiple parents. Substantive Category pages should explain Scope, Includes, Excludes/boundary, role, and related navigation.

=== Concepts / semantic queries ===
Reusable computed sets and queries over canonical SMW state. Membership is projected from assertions rather than copied into a second semantic store.

=== Outlines ===
Hierarchical study maps useful for orientation without claiming the outline is ontology.

=== Lists / registries ===
Useful when individual members need annotation, references, comparison fields, dates, ordering, or status.

=== Contents / indices / glossaries ===
Complementary large-scale orientation, lookup, and vocabulary surfaces.

=== Semantic queries, Lua projections and knowledge graphs ===
Dynamic views over structured properties/relationships. Lua can normalize and compose reusable view models for MediaWiki or BIThub-facing integrations. A graph visualizes knowledge; drawing or deriving a display edge does not make a new stored edge true.

=== Core / Vital coverage ===
A future curated priority layer can identify important missing/weak knowledge without turning importance into semantic identity.

See [[BITwiki:Navigation]].

== Self-modeling substrate ==

BITwiki should be able to describe consequential technologies that make BITwiki possible. Main-space knowledge objects now include [[BITwiki]], [[BIThub]], [[MediaWiki]], [[Discourse]], [[Semantic MediaWiki]], [[Cargo (MediaWiki extension)]], [[Scribunto]], and [[Lua]].

These pages explain the general technology/project while BITwiki-namespace pages explain local implementation policy. Conservative SMW dependency/association assertions make the operating stack queryable as graph state.

== Reader depth / localization ==

Progressive disclosure has an explicit editorial model:

<pre>
Simple orientation
→ Core understanding
→ Technical depth
→ Frontier / research
</pre>

This is primarily depth/readability localization within one canonical knowledge graph. Language translation is a separate dimension. See [[BITwiki:Readable depth and localization]].

== Contribution and administration ==

Missing knowledge should not be represented by generic empty shells. Use [[BITwiki:Requested knowledge]] until a coherent sourced object can be created.

[[BITwiki:Contributing]] defines the contribution path. Architecture, governance, maintenance, validation, research queues, requests, portal/category administration, templates/properties/modules, migrations, automation, BIThub integration, and security belong to project-operational surfaces rather than subject Domains.

== V1 lessons carried forward ==

=== Keep ===

* portals as intelligent entry points;
* modular/transcludable content;
* reusable computational logic where demonstrated;
* semantic relationships;
* knowledge-graph navigation;
* polyhierarchy/faceting where useful;
* progressive disclosure;
* explicit evidence/provenance;
* patterns that emerge from use;
* revisions/redirects preserving continuity.

=== Refactor ===

* category-as-filesystem;
* Topic/domain/entity-type conflation;
* portal hierarchy as ontology;
* one giant taxonomy carrying subject + type + epistemics + workflow;
* numeric confidence as mandatory decoration;
* unimplemented documentation presented as live capability;
* monolithic template/parser-function logic where reusable Lua is clearer;
* duplicate <code>.REVISED</code>/<code>final</code> publication surfaces;
* arbitrary fragments or stubs with no independent knowledge value.

